WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.679
Hello, and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:09.170
we're going to be practicing how to
identify cis and trans alkenes, and

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.169
also going through some naming of
them, um, in this particular video.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.969
So the first thing is, let's just start
with the fundamentals, is how can you

00:00:16.969 --> 00:00:19.640
identify when you have a cis trans alkene?

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:23.630
The whole idea with this is a simple
trick that how I always view these,

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:27.880
is you're going to be looking exactly
at the double bond and pretending that

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.689
it's like a set of railroad tracks.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:34.919
So when I have this little double bond
here, what I always do is I draw in my

00:00:34.919 --> 00:00:39.620
head, or if you want to on your paper,
is a little set of dashed lines, and

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.330
you're going to be pretending that that
double bond is a little railroad track.

00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:48.019
Now what you need to do to determine
if they're cis or trans is you're

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:53.580
looking to see if there are different
groups on each side of the double bond.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:58.925
So for example, if I look at the
CH3s here, We see that both of these

00:00:58.945 --> 00:01:01.585
are on the same side at the moment.

00:01:01.945 --> 00:01:05.545
So whenever we have them both on
the upper or both on the lower

00:01:05.624 --> 00:01:08.875
together, this is referred to as sys.

00:01:09.324 --> 00:01:11.324
Okay, so sys, same.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:15.795
And the entire idea here is that whenever
you have these differences, you need

00:01:15.805 --> 00:01:20.105
to have on each end, there needs to
be two completely different groups.

00:01:20.115 --> 00:01:22.925
So you can see that I have like
the methyl, and I also have the

00:01:22.925 --> 00:01:25.825
hydrogen on the left, they're
both different from each other.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.210
So it needs to be different
on both sides here.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:29.770
Now, both of them are together.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.550
So this is for the methyls.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.060
So this is referred to as cis.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.240
Now, if I go to another one,
well, what would be the other?

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.529
Well, if I have cis, then I also have
trans and I do the same little idea.

00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:44.570
I look and I draw my little dashes
and then I see that, Ooh, okay.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.050
I can have my methyl that one
of them is on the top and then

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.070
the other one is on the bottom.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.840
So we have them on opposite
sides of my railroad track.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:58.565
Now in this case, whenever they are
opposite, this is referred to as trans.

00:01:59.125 --> 00:02:03.845
So whenever it comes to trying to identify
these different structures, We still

00:02:03.845 --> 00:02:06.395
follow the same normal naming conventions.

00:02:06.395 --> 00:02:11.345
We named the longest chain, just like
we would do for any alkane, however,

00:02:11.345 --> 00:02:15.815
or alkene, but we need to make sure
we include that cis trans in the name.

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:20.884
So for example, I have four carbons along
my longest stretch of my chain here.

00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:24.924
So the way that you would name this
one is you would say trans to tell

00:02:24.924 --> 00:02:27.005
you about this isomer here, right?

00:02:27.005 --> 00:02:31.394
That says that we have this three
dimensional shape around this molecule.

00:02:32.015 --> 00:02:36.165
And the way we would number
this, we'd say trans 2 butene.

00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:39.234
Now for the next one, the way
you would name it as well, you

00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:40.444
might be able to kind of guess it.

00:02:40.445 --> 00:02:45.924
Well, if the other one is cis, the other
one would be cis 2 butene for the name.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:46.445
Okay?

00:02:47.154 --> 00:02:51.204
So each one of them says their
particular geometry around it.

00:02:51.265 --> 00:02:52.614
Now why is that the case?

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.510
Well, the thing to keep in
mind is the double bond has a

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.700
trigonal planar geometry to it.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.739
And because of the double bond, it
actually is stuck in that entire geometry.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.159
You can't just simply rotate it without
breaking the bond in the process.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:11.979
So, you know, if you rotate the whole
molecule, those different groups

00:03:11.979 --> 00:03:15.460
are always going to be pointing in
the, always going to be opposite of

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.770
each other no matter what, unless
you break the bond or force it to

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.399
go through a chemical reaction.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.119
So So that's the most important thing
about these is that even if I end up

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:29.290
rotating all these different structures,
they are always going to be stuck in

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.649
that cis or trans unless you go make
it go through an actual reaction.

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.169
So let's take a look at a bunch of
examples of this practicing identifying

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:40.389
sister trans and going through our naming.

00:03:45.649 --> 00:03:46.079
All right.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:49.409
So for the following problem, we're
going to be naming and identifying the

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:54.370
different So with this, the whole idea
first is we need to just go through

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.110
each example and let's try to see if
we can figure out if it's cis or trans.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.740
And then we're going to
feed that into its name.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.579
So the first thing is I'm going to draw
my little railroad tracks just like I

00:04:04.579 --> 00:04:06.349
did in the introduction of this video.

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:10.220
So what we're looking at is are the
different groups, are they on the same

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.270
side or are they on different sides?

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.529
And it doesn't have to only be methyl
groups, it can be anything as long

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:17.770
as it's different carbon groups.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:21.644
halogens, as long as you see them,
are they together or the opposite?

00:04:22.284 --> 00:04:25.825
So we see both these chlorines, they're
both together, so being that they

00:04:25.825 --> 00:04:29.195
are the same, that refers to as cis.

00:04:29.805 --> 00:04:33.394
So what we would do for the naming
is we'd first start by writing that

00:04:33.395 --> 00:04:35.184
down, so we'd have cis in the name.

00:04:35.835 --> 00:04:39.515
Now the next part is going to be
naming and numbering our different

00:04:39.525 --> 00:04:41.885
groups, which are our chlorines here.

00:04:42.364 --> 00:04:46.594
We have two of them, so we've got
to number our, our molecule here,

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.309
They're gonna be at one and two.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:50.950
We'll put a dash.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.729
So one, two, and being that there
are multiple, we'll say die and then

00:04:55.729 --> 00:04:58.070
we will finish it with di Chloro.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:04.550
And then being that we had two carbons,
it's gonna be di chloro ethene for the e

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.250
and e ending to denote the double bond.

00:05:07.819 --> 00:05:08.809
Keep that in mind, right?

00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:12.204
We gotta make sure we do include
that ending when we are doing these.

00:05:12.804 --> 00:05:14.634
Don't accidentally put alkane endings.

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:18.215
It's really easy to do that, especially
when you're first learning all this stuff.

00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:19.465
Okay.

00:05:19.785 --> 00:05:22.375
Now, if we were to do the next one,
same idea here is we're going to

00:05:22.375 --> 00:05:23.855
be looking at the different groups.

00:05:24.525 --> 00:05:27.065
So we see that we have two different ones.

00:05:27.065 --> 00:05:30.844
They don't have to be the same as long
as you're seeing are they opposite?

00:05:31.085 --> 00:05:31.885
Are they together?

00:05:32.284 --> 00:05:35.634
In this case, when I look at my
little railroad track, I see that

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:38.044
they are both opposites of each other.

00:05:38.465 --> 00:05:41.105
So what that means is they
are going to be trans.

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.915
So when it comes to us naming,
we're going to say trans.

00:05:45.604 --> 00:05:49.085
And from there we're going
to number and name all these.

00:05:49.494 --> 00:05:51.534
So how do I decide the numbering here?

00:05:51.915 --> 00:05:54.885
Well, being that you have
two halogen, you gotta go off

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:56.955
electro negativity for the order.

00:05:57.844 --> 00:06:02.345
Chlorine is a higher electro negativity,
so that's gonna get the number one spot.

00:06:03.145 --> 00:06:07.225
Then from there, what we're gonna do
is we're going to, uh, alphabetize

00:06:07.255 --> 00:06:08.455
how they're written in the name.

00:06:08.965 --> 00:06:10.915
This can be trans to bromo.

00:06:12.114 --> 00:06:13.435
Then one chloro.

00:06:15.105 --> 00:06:18.015
And then we'll finish that
name with the ethene part.

00:06:18.015 --> 00:06:19.895
So I'm going to write that
underneath because I kind of

00:06:19.895 --> 00:06:20.965
ran out of room on myself.

00:06:21.685 --> 00:06:27.305
So it'd be trans 2 bromo 1 chloro
ethene would be its entire name.

00:06:28.015 --> 00:06:28.935
Let's try to do another.

00:06:29.695 --> 00:06:31.914
So with this one, is it cis or trans?

00:06:31.914 --> 00:06:32.904
That's just the entire idea.

00:06:33.575 --> 00:06:37.395
So what we need to look at is if you
notice on the left side of this molecule,

00:06:37.735 --> 00:06:41.905
so if I look over here we can see that we
have a hydrogen and then another hydrogen.

00:06:42.345 --> 00:06:44.645
They're both on the exact same side.

00:06:44.915 --> 00:06:49.215
How can you tell the difference
between these two hydrogens?

00:06:49.725 --> 00:06:51.844
And you'll think, well, I can't, right?

00:06:51.845 --> 00:06:53.205
They're the exact same thing.

00:06:53.705 --> 00:06:59.364
So what that means is, well, even though I
have that fluorine, it's gonna be neither.

00:06:59.374 --> 00:07:02.134
So you just read it like
a normal molecule, right?

00:07:02.145 --> 00:07:05.625
We can still go through numbering it
and we could still just say one fluoro

00:07:06.659 --> 00:07:09.670
Athene would be the name of this molecule.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.400
So just like you would name any normal
alkene, they don't all have cis or trans.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.170
The only double bonds that do are
when you have unique groups on

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.880
both sides of that double bond.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:21.200
Okay.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.639
Same idea here is let's do the idea.

00:07:23.639 --> 00:07:25.509
So I did as a skeletal structure here.

00:07:26.089 --> 00:07:28.310
So these are a little bit trickier, right?

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.950
Cause you got to actually remember that
there are hydrogens that are hidden.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:34.620
Um, they're not being showed.

00:07:34.679 --> 00:07:35.730
They're always assumed.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.160
So if you think of like the one on
the left, right, even though they're

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:45.100
not here, there are two hydrogens
that are in existence, right?

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.299
There needs to be four bonds
on every single carbon.

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:50.499
So what does that mean if I look at that?

00:07:50.499 --> 00:07:54.880
Well, I have the same
groups on the left, right?

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:55.779
They're both hydrogens.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.160
But the other thing is I also have
two of the same groups on the other

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.020
side, What are each of the ends here?

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:03.080
These are CH3s.

00:08:04.705 --> 00:08:08.105
So these are identical as
well, so they're no different.

00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:13.934
So meaning that when we see this, it has
neither, it has nothing, it has no cis or

00:08:13.934 --> 00:08:16.945
trans, it's just a normal, uh, structure.

00:08:17.395 --> 00:08:19.245
So how would we go through
numbering this, right?

00:08:19.245 --> 00:08:24.474
So we go one, two, and three, so
therefore making it 2 methylpentene

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:31.425
would be my name, or not pentene,
propene would be my name, okay?

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:34.414
Let's do a couple more,
and then we'll be done.

00:08:35.164 --> 00:08:39.155
So now this next one, same idea as
before, we want to name and number it and

00:08:39.155 --> 00:08:40.505
then look at all the different groups.

00:08:41.005 --> 00:08:44.934
Now with these guys, when it
comes to cis or trans, these are

00:08:44.934 --> 00:08:49.264
something that you cannot actually
label with the cis or trans system.

00:08:49.814 --> 00:08:54.355
Um, if you're someone that's taking this
as an organic chemistry major, this is

00:08:54.355 --> 00:08:57.495
where E and Z notation would come from.

00:08:57.785 --> 00:09:00.845
This is something that is
not covered in my course.

00:09:00.855 --> 00:09:03.035
That's for more for
allied health students.

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:06.810
where you're trying to assign
priority to everything.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.770
So in this case, there's no
exact way for you to tell

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.150
what's the most important thing.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.420
You can say that the chlorines
are opposite of each other.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.970
So there could be an argument that
could be made to say that this is trans.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.040
Same thing with the carbon groups
being opposite of each other.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:28.730
However, I think it's more appropriate to
say that you can't really label this one.

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.430
It would be better to use a
different notation for this.

00:09:31.985 --> 00:09:35.095
So we're just going to go through and
just name, practice, name your alkene,

00:09:35.145 --> 00:09:38.375
and we're going to say, we're going to do
kind of like neither, but we're going to

00:09:38.375 --> 00:09:45.494
put a little asterisk next to it that you
could say cis or trans, but I would say

00:09:45.675 --> 00:09:52.044
it probably be better not to, um, without
doing a priority based naming system.

00:09:52.835 --> 00:09:56.415
So with this guy, right, when we were
trying to name it, so I kind of like

00:09:56.415 --> 00:09:57.985
numbered it a little bit off, right?

00:09:57.985 --> 00:10:01.814
So we'd say it'd be one over here on
the methyl, and we have number two.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.060
And then we'd have 3, 4, and 5, right?

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.450
So we would just kind of shift
all of our numbers down, right?

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.960
We want to make sure we have
that longest continuous chain.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.620
It's really easy to forget that when you
are dealing with these kinds of molecules.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:20.420
So what we would have is we
have 2, 3, dichlorohexene

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.350
in this case.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.140
So we number where those dichloros are.

